1
00:00:04,874 --> 00:00:06,935
[SPEAKER_00]: So, here's a question which frequently comes up.

2
00:00:08,937 --> 00:00:15,882
[SPEAKER_00]: There's this idea in your teachings that patiently enduring mental pressures is what purifies the mind from defilements.

3
00:00:17,203 --> 00:00:24,389
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's hard to square this with how the Buddha often encouraged disciples to be diligent and strive to abandon unwholesome states.

4
00:00:25,450 --> 00:00:28,332
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, that's a simile about your hair being on fire and so on.

5
00:00:29,452 --> 00:00:45,840
[SPEAKER_00]: So, one cannot deny that enduring certain things is an important part of the training, but if that's all you do, and you never make an active effort to remove what is unwholesome from your mind, then how can it ever truly become pure?

6
00:00:47,020 --> 00:00:47,961
[SPEAKER_00]: What would you say?

7
00:00:48,441 --> 00:00:50,162
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a frequent question which we hear.

8
00:00:50,402 --> 00:00:50,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

9
00:00:51,062 --> 00:00:57,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the confusion comes from the person's understanding of what an unwholesome state is.

10
00:00:58,267 --> 00:01:03,550
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the natural tendency is like, you know, you come from the starting point.

11
00:01:04,770 --> 00:01:10,854
[SPEAKER_01]: What you have is, basically, your mind revolving around certain thoughts.

12
00:01:11,815 --> 00:01:17,218
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, memories of sensual things that you experienced in the past, or things you want.

13
00:01:17,978 --> 00:01:20,419
[SPEAKER_01]: In the future, or somebody upsets you, or something.

14
00:01:21,260 --> 00:01:35,147
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the easy assumption to make is that, oh, well, if I just stop these thoughts themselves from coming up... Well, first of all, you associate that with the unwholesome state.

15
00:01:35,647 --> 00:01:41,990
[SPEAKER_01]: It's these thoughts, that if I can just somehow deal with them, suppress them, my mind will be pure.

16
00:01:42,831 --> 00:01:47,193
[SPEAKER_01]: But what you fail to realize is, somebody who has that view, is that

17
00:01:48,637 --> 00:01:53,999
[SPEAKER_01]: The actual defilement, like the greed, aversion, delusion... Like, what is greed?

18
00:01:54,099 --> 00:01:56,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Greed is leaning towards pleasure.

19
00:01:57,160 --> 00:02:00,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Aversion is leaning away from displeasure.

20
00:02:01,142 --> 00:02:12,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Delusion or distraction is kind of, yeah, leaning away from the neutrality of the neutral feeling, because, you know, it's somehow not enough in and of itself.

21
00:02:13,787 --> 00:02:21,218
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you realize, well, the defilement is not the presence of pleasure, the presence of pain.

22
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:27,968
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your inability to... well, your tendency to want to act out of it, let's say.

23
00:02:29,069 --> 00:02:51,944
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, when you have these, you know, kind of persistent pressures in your mind, instead of blaming those things, which is kind of what that type of view comes from, it's like, even if you don't think of it that way, you imply that through getting rid of the objects, by focusing on your breath or whatever,

24
00:02:53,025 --> 00:02:54,186
[SPEAKER_01]: You're fixing the problem.

25
00:02:54,586 --> 00:03:12,757
[SPEAKER_01]: But no, it's like, the problem will be fixed, actually, when, in regard to those same objects and those same thoughts, there is no movement, no internal kind of need to get them, if they're pleasant, to run away from them, if they're unpleasant.

26
00:03:13,897 --> 00:03:20,462
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that should be your aim, that should be your measure for what development is.

27
00:03:21,022 --> 00:03:31,569
[SPEAKER_01]: Because otherwise, it's like, you know, you can say whatever you want, that you can jump super high when there's no gravity anymore.

28
00:03:32,830 --> 00:03:33,790
[SPEAKER_01]: But what's the point of that?

29
00:03:34,831 --> 00:03:42,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, what's the point of that your mind can be peaceful when these things are not there, when you have suppressed those pressures?

30
00:03:43,074 --> 00:03:44,795
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's a very circumstantial piece now.

31
00:03:44,915 --> 00:03:45,276
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

32
00:03:45,356 --> 00:03:48,098
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you want to recognize, like, if you...

33
00:03:48,358 --> 00:04:00,708
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, even if you think about it just on the level of how the world works, there is no way that you will permanently be able to shelter yourself from these things, like...

34
00:04:01,348 --> 00:04:02,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Things happen.

35
00:04:03,609 --> 00:04:06,470
[SPEAKER_01]: Unexpected situations come up.

36
00:04:06,750 --> 00:04:21,616
[SPEAKER_01]: As much sense restraint as you might have been practicing, things will come through sometimes, that will bring up those kind of minds spinning around this or that.

37
00:04:21,856 --> 00:04:28,218
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you have to recognize, wait, no, the problem is not the object, as the Buddha said,

38
00:04:29,959 --> 00:04:33,526
[SPEAKER_01]: Beautiful things in the world are not what sanctity is.

39
00:04:33,546 --> 00:04:37,614
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your lustful intention in regard to those things that constitutes sanctity.

40
00:04:38,175 --> 00:04:39,939
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, that's the whole...

41
00:04:41,761 --> 00:04:50,767
[SPEAKER_01]: This whole need to discern the sign of your mind, because then you start to recognize, well, it has nothing to do with these objects.

42
00:04:50,987 --> 00:04:54,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, these objects can remain there forever, if they want.

43
00:04:55,270 --> 00:05:05,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, if you interpret that statement to mean, oh, well, the beautiful things remain there in the world, meaning out there, in like the external, in the city or something.

44
00:05:05,897 --> 00:05:16,604
[SPEAKER_01]: But no, it's the internal... the world of your own senses and sense objects, like these things, as the suttas would say, they belong to Mara, and Mara is in control of them.

45
00:05:17,305 --> 00:05:22,588
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, on that internal level, that's what the world is, and these things will remain there.

46
00:05:23,689 --> 00:05:26,152
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot... to some extent, they will remain there.

47
00:05:26,192 --> 00:05:40,488
[SPEAKER_01]: You can be sense-restrained, and you should be, but sense-restraint is not about, like... unfortunately, that's how it's interpreted often, but it's not about preventing these things as much as you can, as a blanket.

48
00:05:41,029 --> 00:05:55,229
[SPEAKER_01]: effort it's about well when when the seeking of these things would be for me rooted in sensuality i don't do so when the kind of um focusing on the

49
00:05:58,031 --> 00:06:04,393
[SPEAKER_01]: This unsatisfying aspect of these things, when that would be rooted in aversion, I don't attend in that direction.

50
00:06:04,994 --> 00:06:18,540
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that is literally how the suttas describe sense-restraint, because it already comes from that understanding where you don't blame the objects themselves, and even the feelings that they may arouse in you.

51
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:21,361
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't blame that for the problem.

52
00:06:21,701 --> 00:06:24,743
[SPEAKER_01]: You actually see your mind, and you see this

53
00:06:25,623 --> 00:06:29,246
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, need to act out as the actual problem.

54
00:06:29,846 --> 00:06:41,774
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by enduring the pressure, you're actually enduring the pressure which requires you to be, you know, keeping the precepts and and practicing sensory strength.

55
00:06:41,794 --> 00:06:49,119
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it doesn't mean that when you endure the pressure, you're kind of letting your mind indulge in these thoughts.

56
00:06:49,400 --> 00:06:50,300
[SPEAKER_01]: It's quite the opposite.

57
00:06:50,640 --> 00:06:52,242
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like you you will have to learn

58
00:06:53,162 --> 00:06:57,864
[SPEAKER_01]: To rein the mind back in, in order to start enduring these things properly.

59
00:06:59,064 --> 00:07:17,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you are welcoming the sensual thoughts, welcoming the thoughts of ill-will, picking them up, revolving around them, you know, proliferating these narratives and things that your mind will be trying to push on you, you're not enduring it for that very reason.

60
00:07:17,471 --> 00:07:19,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you're going the path of least resistance.

61
00:07:19,698 --> 00:07:22,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so there is such a thing as mental restraint as well.

62
00:07:22,265 --> 00:07:24,330
[SPEAKER_00]: So, earlier you were referring about Marwa.

63
00:07:24,350 --> 00:07:27,197
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it doesn't really matter where you go, because...

64
00:07:27,872 --> 00:07:30,834
[SPEAKER_01]: You're enclosed within his range.

65
00:07:30,894 --> 00:07:37,959
[SPEAKER_00]: Within his range, which includes also thoughts as the objects of the sixth sense.

66
00:07:38,319 --> 00:07:44,263
[SPEAKER_01]: And it doesn't even matter which thought you choose, because the craving, as the suttas say, it will land anywhere.

67
00:07:44,924 --> 00:07:49,027
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you just pick an object that you're kind of...

68
00:07:50,007 --> 00:07:54,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Intellectual reasoning tells you is less bad than the others.

69
00:07:54,931 --> 00:07:59,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you will be, as people would often do, like, oh, the breath is a neutral object.

70
00:07:59,375 --> 00:08:03,698
[SPEAKER_01]: Therefore, if I just focus on this, I will be taking my mind away from the bad stuff.

71
00:08:03,778 --> 00:08:09,182
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's, again, completely missing the point that it's not the stuff

72
00:08:10,063 --> 00:08:11,864
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the reason for your craving.

73
00:08:12,404 --> 00:08:20,567
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that internal attitude of your mind that will shift towards anything in the entire universe, regardless of its of its content.

74
00:08:20,607 --> 00:08:31,952
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you see that that is the the source of the whole thing, then you realize, well, replacing the object makes absolutely no no difference.

75
00:08:32,112 --> 00:08:36,036
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you could have a sensual mind, even in relation to just breathing in and breathing out.

76
00:08:36,477 --> 00:08:41,743
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensual, or you could say passionate mind, like a mind that is dependent on something.

77
00:08:42,884 --> 00:08:43,745
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is the problem.

78
00:08:44,266 --> 00:08:49,291
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the thing that causes you to be subjected to suffering, that dependence.

79
00:08:49,992 --> 00:08:53,116
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you realize that actually, if you were to

80
00:08:54,917 --> 00:08:57,858
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's say, that's why it's the middle way.

81
00:08:58,558 --> 00:09:13,023
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't allow yourself to give in to the obvious path of indulgence, but you also don't go in the opposite direction of trying to remove the things that are trying to bait you.

82
00:09:13,943 --> 00:09:20,546
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where you find that middle way, which is where you neither push forward nor stand still, as the suttas would say.

83
00:09:21,687 --> 00:09:32,992
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you have the pressuring thing there, but you are rightly recognizing where the line is and where your

84
00:09:34,393 --> 00:09:37,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Engagement and picking up of those baits actually begins.

85
00:09:38,054 --> 00:09:38,234
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

86
00:09:38,474 --> 00:09:47,596
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, by turning yourself to stay on that level, you're actually taming permanently, forever, what the lust actually is.

87
00:09:47,676 --> 00:09:51,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you will not be able to prevent these things from baiting you.

88
00:09:51,697 --> 00:09:54,438
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the Buddha, after his awakening, would say for the

89
00:09:55,958 --> 00:10:00,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Seven years following his awakening, Mara was still trying to gain access to him.

90
00:10:01,182 --> 00:10:07,727
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could see, even after becoming an arahant, you wouldn't be able to stop this, these invitations of Mara from coming.

91
00:10:08,547 --> 00:10:16,513
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if you fully discern that kind of being an island unto yourself, as the suttas would say, that's how you develop it, by

92
00:10:17,233 --> 00:10:27,601
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding, well, it doesn't matter how much this stuff comes up through eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, mind.

93
00:10:28,661 --> 00:10:33,865
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter what things are being presented through those six gateways.

94
00:10:34,986 --> 00:10:39,949
[SPEAKER_01]: It's my choice to cultivate certain attitudes in regard to those things.

95
00:10:41,410 --> 00:10:45,433
[SPEAKER_01]: It's my choice not to want to endure them.

96
00:10:45,673 --> 00:10:54,359
[SPEAKER_01]: And, in fact, there is one sutta where it explicitly says, who is the one who has gone beyond, who has crossed over to the farther shore?

97
00:10:54,960 --> 00:11:03,926
[SPEAKER_01]: And it says, it's the one who can endure the pressure of the eye, of the ear, of the nose, of the tongue, of the body, of the mind, of mano.

98
00:11:04,066 --> 00:11:06,888
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I think it also says that that's necessary to gain jhāna.

99
00:11:07,244 --> 00:11:08,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

100
00:11:08,925 --> 00:11:27,435
[SPEAKER_01]: So, because, well, that's a whole other rabbit hole, but the view of what jhāna is nowadays precisely reflects that wrong view of people thinking that it's the object that the mind happens to associate the pressure with.

101
00:11:28,556 --> 00:11:36,343
[SPEAKER_01]: By removing the object, I supposedly fix the problem and I enter this transient state of almost being on vacation.

102
00:11:36,923 --> 00:11:51,536
[SPEAKER_01]: But the actual jhāna would be when, in fact, the suttas describe it that way as well, in regard to pleasant sights, pleasant sounds, pleasant smells, tastes and touches, those remain there.

103
00:11:52,610 --> 00:11:54,212
[SPEAKER_01]: The same place where they're always been.

104
00:11:54,853 --> 00:11:58,017
[SPEAKER_01]: But internally, there is no more passion towards those things.

105
00:11:58,778 --> 00:12:01,742
[SPEAKER_01]: And for that, you need to allow those things to arise.

106
00:12:01,822 --> 00:12:06,668
[SPEAKER_01]: How will you develop this passion towards this, if this is not present?

107
00:12:07,100 --> 00:12:20,309
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but this now doesn't mean that somebody needs to deliberately seek out provocative sites in order to tame their lust or their aversion, because this is on a much more general level.

108
00:12:20,389 --> 00:12:22,591
[SPEAKER_00]: You already have those sense bases.

109
00:12:22,611 --> 00:12:24,332
[SPEAKER_00]: There is already experience going on.

110
00:12:24,412 --> 00:12:25,953
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot not be like that.

111
00:12:26,073 --> 00:12:26,253
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

112
00:12:26,654 --> 00:12:28,855
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if somebody thinks, oh, I want to test myself.

113
00:12:28,915 --> 00:12:34,019
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's go back to a more, let's say, urban environment and see how my mind reacts.

114
00:12:34,059 --> 00:12:35,920
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already entirely missing the point now.

115
00:12:36,074 --> 00:12:46,597
[SPEAKER_01]: No, and that's why the whole training of the middle way that we described, the foremost way to do that, is the monk life.

116
00:12:47,517 --> 00:12:49,758
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like living away from these things.

117
00:12:50,278 --> 00:12:53,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Ironically, that's how you would be able to properly face them.

118
00:12:53,239 --> 00:12:53,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

119
00:12:53,759 --> 00:13:05,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, if you were exposing yourself to them, that view already, again, you're putting the emphasis on the objects, and you're overlooking the fact that, more often than not,

120
00:13:08,052 --> 00:13:17,836
[SPEAKER_01]: The very attitude of your mind towards the objects that's getting you to go there, in the first place, basically, the passion is already there.

121
00:13:17,916 --> 00:13:22,318
[SPEAKER_01]: The perception of these things as gratifying is already there.

122
00:13:22,438 --> 00:13:33,542
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, you might be kind of training some kind of willpower to say no to the wild animal that you've made wilder by going there, but you're certainly not decreasing the wildness of the animal.

123
00:13:36,574 --> 00:13:45,657
[SPEAKER_01]: You actually have to go the opposite way of keeping the precepts, practice in sense restraint, as we said before, but then, you know...

124
00:13:47,425 --> 00:13:52,366
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't keep kind of running as far as you can in that direction, because that's another trap.

125
00:13:52,946 --> 00:14:02,269
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like thinking, the more I restrain, the less kind of contact I have with these things, the more secluded from passion I will be.

126
00:14:03,049 --> 00:14:04,249
[SPEAKER_01]: And that would be another mistake.

127
00:14:04,569 --> 00:14:06,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, now you're going too far in the opposite direction.

128
00:14:07,310 --> 00:14:14,012
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you keep the precepts and you establish yourself in this container of, you know, a certain

129
00:14:15,172 --> 00:14:23,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Diminished exposure to these things, but then, within that, even within that, they will still be coming up.

130
00:14:23,077 --> 00:14:24,657
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is where you have to allow them?

131
00:14:24,778 --> 00:14:25,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

132
00:14:25,278 --> 00:14:38,905
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where, like, being fully established in the precepts, being fully established in sense restraint, in wakefulness, in clear comprehension, as we describe it,

133
00:14:40,706 --> 00:14:49,871
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you're in a position to actually abandon the five hindrances, where you would be neither pushing, like, neither kind of...

134
00:14:51,267 --> 00:15:05,800
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not letting yourself go with the momentum of these thoughts, which is what you would be doing before, nor are you doing what most people think practice is, which is to try to suppress those objects as as much as you can.

135
00:15:06,580 --> 00:15:13,747
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you start to realize that middleman there, which is where the citta actually is,

136
00:15:14,687 --> 00:15:25,671
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, you realize, yeah, I can have the exact same object, completely unchanged, but the citta internally has no interest in it anymore.

137
00:15:26,251 --> 00:15:27,932
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the same significance is there.

138
00:15:28,592 --> 00:15:35,054
[SPEAKER_01]: The same... like, you would clearly recognize this is the exact same thing that was arousing lust in the past.

139
00:15:35,654 --> 00:15:37,235
[SPEAKER_01]: You haven't, like...

140
00:15:37,335 --> 00:15:41,779
[SPEAKER_01]: Turn your gaze away from it, it's still there, and you see, but now there is no lust.

141
00:15:42,320 --> 00:15:45,042
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what the suttas would call the opening amidst confinement.

142
00:15:45,863 --> 00:15:56,694
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, in regard to the things that continue to be pleasant, continue to be beautiful, you haven't, like, put a mask on them, so that, you know, the animal doesn't react.

143
00:15:56,794 --> 00:15:59,877
[SPEAKER_01]: No, the animal has become dispassionate towards the same thing.

144
00:16:00,637 --> 00:16:00,978
[SPEAKER_01]: And then,

145
00:16:02,039 --> 00:16:17,450
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where you start to realize why the Buddha used these similes of the clever cook having to pick up the signs of the king to describe this principle of recognizing your mind, i.e.

146
00:16:18,011 --> 00:16:23,255
[SPEAKER_01]: recognizing where those unwholesome states actually are, going back to that original question.

147
00:16:23,295 --> 00:16:27,278
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not something that you would be able to spot by just looking.

148
00:16:27,378 --> 00:16:29,400
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I have such thoughts arising in my mind?

149
00:16:30,220 --> 00:16:35,164
[SPEAKER_01]: This thing clearly is what the problem is, and that's where it ends.

150
00:16:35,324 --> 00:16:45,811
[SPEAKER_01]: If it were that easy, then, you know, as the Buddha said that the whole dhamma, he didn't want to teach it, because it would be too subtle, and it would go over people's heads.

151
00:16:46,952 --> 00:16:47,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Precisely because of this.

152
00:16:48,113 --> 00:16:56,519
[SPEAKER_01]: Because even when the untrained mind tries to do something about the problem, it aims at the objects, aims at the

153
00:16:59,088 --> 00:17:03,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that is where it always was, due to not having restrained the sensuality first.

154
00:17:03,790 --> 00:17:03,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

155
00:17:04,471 --> 00:17:06,271
[SPEAKER_01]: That's always where your emphasis was, exactly.

156
00:17:06,291 --> 00:17:13,014
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's just a continuation of the same tendency, the same wrong view that you would have had.

157
00:17:13,035 --> 00:17:24,640
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why the self-mortification, which you could summarize as the tendency to put the blame on the objects and try to get rid of them as intensely as possible,

158
00:17:25,400 --> 00:17:28,221
[SPEAKER_01]: That actually keeps you within the exact same domain.

159
00:17:28,541 --> 00:17:31,161
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that it's taking you far, farther away from sensuality.

160
00:17:31,641 --> 00:17:32,542
[SPEAKER_01]: It kind of brings you back.

161
00:17:32,982 --> 00:17:39,083
[SPEAKER_01]: By trying to go too far, you actually return to the same, to the same domain of Mara.

162
00:17:39,803 --> 00:17:48,225
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's that whole sutta where the deer herd, it says like they, by trying to run away from the hunting ground,

163
00:17:49,560 --> 00:17:58,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Eventually, they end up equally caught as the first deer... herd of deer that were just carelessly eating everything in that hunting ground.

164
00:17:58,968 --> 00:18:06,574
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the fourth herd of deer, the smart ones, what they realize is like, wait, we don't need to run away from this stuff.

165
00:18:07,755 --> 00:18:13,419
[SPEAKER_01]: We don't need to try to hide because, like, you know, the hunter factually could be anywhere.

166
00:18:14,340 --> 00:18:15,241
[SPEAKER_01]: What we have to do is,

167
00:18:17,445 --> 00:18:25,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Exercise mindfulness, but of the right kind, in regard to the things that are inside that domain.

168
00:18:25,667 --> 00:18:30,529
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, going back to what we just said, don't blame the object.

169
00:18:31,869 --> 00:18:34,050
[SPEAKER_01]: See what is that internal attitude

170
00:18:35,051 --> 00:18:36,812
[SPEAKER_01]: of your citta in regard to it.

171
00:18:36,972 --> 00:18:42,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there an obsessive kind of like impulsive tendency towards it?

172
00:18:43,377 --> 00:18:51,484
[SPEAKER_01]: And you would see that kind of thing could come and go from day to day, which already starts to show you that in regard to the same thing.

173
00:18:51,644 --> 00:18:52,624
[SPEAKER_00]: You mean the impulsivity?

174
00:18:52,664 --> 00:18:53,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

175
00:18:53,164 --> 00:19:03,948
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it already starts to show you, well, if truly the problem was on this level, then this would have to remain the same, always, in regard to this object.

176
00:19:04,108 --> 00:19:17,192
[SPEAKER_01]: But now you realize, that kind of thing that's sometimes like this, sometimes like that, while everything else remains the same, that already starts to show you those indirect signs of that citta.

177
00:19:18,292 --> 00:19:31,923
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where you would be able to turn it away, completely, to the opposite direction, without actually moving an inch, in terms of the place where you were, basically.

178
00:19:32,023 --> 00:19:33,625
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's kind of like...

179
00:19:34,846 --> 00:19:45,995
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, such a subtle thing, because, again, naturally you think, I just run, I just move away from this stuff that's provoking me, and that's how I will be free from it.

180
00:19:46,615 --> 00:20:00,847
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's actually right where you are, but in that level of like the domain of Mara, right where you are, that's where internally the transformation happens, on the level of your citta.

